window.CATALOG_INDEX = [{"title": "Clinic Essentials", "url": "manuals/clinic-essentials/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "clinic fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "← All manuals Clinic Resources · In Preparation Clinic Essentials Practical resources for the epilepsy clinic. In preparation. Trial referral information is drafted; other topics remain in preparation. Clinical review is pending."}, {"heading": "Psychiatry & Mental Health Referral Information", "anchor": "psychiatry-mental-health-title", "text": "Psychiatry & Mental Health Referral Information Epic referral steps, patient selection, consultation scope, and capacity notes for the dedicated epilepsy Psychiatry pathway. Open referral guide"}, {"heading": "Drug Rep & Device Rep Contacts", "anchor": "rep-contacts-title", "text": "Drug Rep & Device Rep Contacts Coming soon."}, {"heading": "Ketogenic Diet Information", "anchor": "ketogenic-diet-title", "text": "Ketogenic Diet Information Johns Hopkins Adult Epilepsy Diet Center — adult dietary therapy program; see the adult section for program and appointment information. SM Keto Nutrition / Sarah Metzger Nutrition — virtual medical ketogenic diet counseling, including epilepsy. Suggested as an Atlanta-area resource; confirm availability for patients in Georgia directly."}, {"heading": "Smartphrases & Templates", "anchor": "smartphrases-templates-title", "text": "Smartphrases & Templates EPIJOBASSIST — Job Assistance Program referrals. EPIPSYCHIATRIST — Psychiatry referrals; also includes a couple of therapy referrals. EPISSDIAPP — Social Security Disability application instructions. SmartPhrase names and descriptions supplied September 25, 2026. Full phrase text has not yet been supplied; this is a directory of available phrases."}, {"heading": "How to Order ASM — The Easy Way", "anchor": "asm-ordering-title", "text": "How to Order ASM — The Easy Way Coming soon."}]}, {"title": "DBS On/Off Quick Reference", "url": "manuals/dbs-on-off/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "stim fellow diane", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Clinical Education Manual DBS On/Off Quick Reference Medtronic DBS clinician programmer workflow for turning therapy off and back on, derived from a bedside teaching demonstration. Draft for colleague review — not approved institutional policy. Training aid only. Use current device labeling, MRI eligibility workflow, patient-specific orders, and local policy. If any screen, device model, or safety status differs from this demo, stop and escalate to the DBS clinician/device representative."}, {"heading": "One-Page Workflow", "anchor": "one-page-workflow", "text": "One-Page Workflow Step Action 1. Start the tablet and open the Medtronic DBS clinician programmer app. 2. Connect to the patient by selecting Connect and turning on the communicator. 3. Position the communicator over the implanted generator and keep it in place for the older device shown. 4. Review impedance guidance: green/OK, yellow/Investigate, red/Avoid. 5. Check impedance on both sides/leads as applicable and confirm the values are acceptable. 6. Turn stimulation Off from the stimulation control. 7. Open End Session , confirm the session is complete, and exit. Stop / Escalate Reason Impedance is red or marked Avoid. In the teaching demonstration, MRI is not allowed if a contact appears broken/red. Communicator will not connect. The older device in the demo requires direct physical placement over the implanted generator; newer devices may connect by Bluetooth. Wrong group/program is selected. Before leaving therapy off or turning it back on, verify the selected group is the intended one."}, {"heading": "Detailed Walkthrough", "anchor": "detailed-walkthrough", "text": "Detailed Walkthrough"}, {"heading": "1. Start the tablet and open DBS", "anchor": "1-start-the-tablet-and-open-dbs", "text": "1. Start the tablet and open DBS Open the DBS clinician programmer screen and start from the main DBS menu. Start at the DBS clinician programmer home screen."}, {"heading": "2. Connect to the patient", "anchor": "2-connect-to-the-patient", "text": "2. Connect to the patient Select Connect and turn on the communicator. The light indicates the communicator is powered and ready to connect. Turn on the communicator, then connect from the DBS app."}, {"heading": "3. Position the communicator", "anchor": "3-position-the-communicator", "text": "3. Position the communicator For the older device shown, place the communicator directly over the implanted generator and keep it positioned while interrogating or changing therapy. Keep the communicator physically positioned over the implanted generator."}, {"heading": "4. Impedance guidance", "anchor": "4-impedance-guidance", "text": "4. Impedance guidance Review the impedance guidance screen. Green/OK is within normal range; yellow/Investigate needs review; red/Avoid suggests a possible fault condition. Use the guidance screen to interpret impedance status."}, {"heading": "5. Impedance - check both sides", "anchor": "5-impedance-check-both-sides", "text": "5. Impedance - check both sides Check the impedance screen for both sides/leads as applicable. In this demo, the green checks indicate acceptable values. Confirm both sides/leads show acceptable impedance results."}, {"heading": "6. Turn stimulation off", "anchor": "6-turn-stimulation-off", "text": "6. Turn stimulation off Use the stimulation control to switch therapy off. Confirm the control reflects the intended off state before proceeding. Switch stimulation off from the stimulation control."}, {"heading": "7. End session", "anchor": "7-end-session", "text": "7. End session Open End Session , confirm the session changes, and exit the session. Use End Session to close out after confirming the therapy state."}, {"heading": "Turning Stimulation Back On", "anchor": "turning-stimulation-back-on", "text": "Turning Stimulation Back On The turn-on process is the same connection workflow in reverse: reconnect to the device, verify the intended group/program, then switch stimulation to On. The teaching demonstration emphasizes correcting the selected group before leaving the patient set up for the next turn-on. Check What to confirm Correct patient/device connection The programmer has connected/interrogated the implanted DBS device. Correct group/program selected The boxed or selected group is the one intended for the patient when therapy is restored. Stimulation status Switch stimulation to On, then confirm the programmer reflects the intended on status before ending the session. Clinical context Confirm whether this is post-MRI restoration, clinic programming, or another supervised workflow."}, {"heading": "Important Teaching Notes From The Video", "anchor": "important-teaching-notes-from-the-video", "text": "Important Teaching Notes From The Video Older vs newer devices: the demonstrated older device requires direct communicator placement over the generator; newer devices may use Bluetooth. MRI safety: The teaching demonstration states that if impedance/contact status is red, MRI should not proceed because this may indicate a broken contact. Group selection matters: before turning off for MRI or preparing for later turn-on, make sure the intended group/program is selected. Programming details are separate: the video includes discussion of double monopolar settings, volts vs milliamps, and cycling intervals. Those details should not be treated as a generic programming protocol."}]}, {"title": "Diane’s Pearls", "url": "manuals/dianes-pearls/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "diane fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "← All manuals Clinical Pearls Diane’s Pearls Practical clinical and workflow pearls from Diane, organized by topic."}, {"heading": "Kaiser Permanente", "anchor": "kaiser-heading", "text": "Kaiser Permanente EMU discharge Medication coverage for patients discharged from the EMU. Reported coverage experience — confirm the patient’s plan. Shared by the user on September 14, 2026; the original coverage decision date and benefit plan were not supplied. These notes are not a prescribing protocol or a universal Kaiser formulary rule. Confirm current coverage and authorization requirements with the dispensing pharmacy."}, {"heading": "Rescue medication coverage", "anchor": "rescue-medication-coverage", "text": "Rescue medication coverage Diane’s report Diane reported that Nayzilam was considered non-formulary and non-preferred and was not approved in the coverage situation described. Diazepam rectal gel: reported as preferred, with a supplied dose range of 2.5–20 mg as needed. This range records the source note; the individual dose must come from the patient’s prescribed rescue plan. If rectal diazepam is not tolerated: Diane reported Valtoco (diazepam nasal spray) as the preferred non-formulary alternative. The supplied note lists 5 mg or 10 mg as one spray in one nostril, and 15 mg or 20 mg total as one spray in each nostril using the two-device dose pack, as needed according to the prescribed rescue plan. The single-device versus two-device distinction matches the Valtoco prescribing information . This pearl does not specify patient selection, repeat dosing, or a complete rescue plan."}, {"heading": "Fintepla authorization", "anchor": "fintepla-authorization", "text": "Fintepla authorization Diane’s report Fintepla approval requires a prior trial of Epidiolex."}, {"heading": "Coverage verification", "anchor": "coverage-verification", "text": "Coverage verification Source check Kaiser Georgia publishes separate formularies by benefit plan . Its 2025 State Employee Health Benefit formulary lists Nayzilam at tier 3 with step therapy, so the reported denial should not be generalized to all Kaiser coverage. That historical document does not establish current coverage for an individual patient."}]}, {"title": "What to Send to Whom", "url": "manuals/document-routing/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Clinic Essentials · Document Routing What to Send to Whom Route letters, forms, and orders to the appropriate team."}, {"heading": "Rashunda / Admin", "anchor": "rashunda-admin", "text": "Rashunda / Admin Letters — except medication and court letters; see Faith’s specific categories below. Driving forms MARTA Mobility forms Parking permits VA forms"}, {"heading": "Faith / Social Worker", "anchor": "faith-social-worker", "text": "Faith / Social Worker Return to work / FMLA Short-term disability (STD) and long-term disability (LTD) leave Social Security Disability (SSDI) letters and forms Citizenship forms ALF / DMA 6 ADA reasonable accommodations Diagnosis (DX) letters Court letters"}, {"heading": "RN / LPN", "anchor": "rn-lpn", "text": "RN / LPN Medication letters OT / PT / SLP / home health orders Seizure protocol forms Medication authorization / medication assistance forms Wheelchair evaluations"}, {"heading": "Letter categories", "anchor": "letter-categories", "text": "Letter categories The supplied list assigns general letters to Admin, medication letters to nursing, and court letters to Faith. It also assigns diagnosis and SSDI letters to Faith. Confirm the recipient when a letter falls into more than one category. Source: division routing list supplied September 25, 2026. “Accept Medication and Court” was interpreted as “except medication and court.” Staff assignments should be confirmed when they change."}]}, {"title": "Inpatient", "url": "manuals/inpatient/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "inpatient EMU", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Inpatient · EMU Resources Inpatient Protocols, on-call workflows, seizure clipping, and stimulation manuals for the inpatient epilepsy team."}, {"heading": "Manuals & Workflows", "anchor": "manuals-workflows", "text": "Manuals & Workflows 05 Cortical Stimulation · Educational Review"}, {"heading": "Cortical Stimulation Manual", "anchor": "cortical-stimulation-manual", "text": "Cortical Stimulation Manual Comprehensive 17-chapter reading edition on cortical stimulation during SEEG: anatomy, responses, interpretation, and source-linked teaching. Draft for colleague review; clinical signoff pending. sEEG stim fellow Open manual EMU Stimulation Manual"}, {"heading": "Electrical Stimulation Mapping SOP", "anchor": "electrical-stimulation-mapping-sop", "text": "Electrical Stimulation Mapping SOP Policy and procedure manual for electrical stimulation mapping during intracranial EEG recordings. sEEG stim fellow Open PDF EMU Workflow Manual"}, {"heading": "How to Clip Seizures", "anchor": "how-to-clip-seizures", "text": "How to Clip Seizures Seizure clipping in Natus, including start and end markers, clip naming, and epoch selection. fellow Open EMU Stimulation Manual"}, {"heading": "Thalamic Protocol", "anchor": "thalamic-protocol", "text": "Thalamic Protocol Protocol/manual for setting up and running thalamic stimulation workflow steps in the EMU context. sEEG stim fellow Open PDF EMU Operations Resource"}, {"heading": "Weekend On Call", "anchor": "weekend-on-call", "text": "Weekend On Call Synergy orders, ReAct referrals, provider responsibilities, NP handoff, and faxing during extended NP absence. oncall fellow Open"}]}, {"title": "Clinical Trials and Projects", "url": "manuals/ongoing-trials/", "kind": "trial", "tags": "clinic fellow research", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Research · Trials & Projects Clinical Trials and Projects Who to consider, what participation involves, and whom to contact."}, {"heading": "Contact Zeke", "anchor": "contact-zeke", "text": "Contact Zeke Zeke Gleichgerrcht · eze.gleich@emory.edu For trial interest, potential candidates, and clinical or protocol questions. Clinician review draft. Local status labels and practical details come from Zeke’s email supplied September 24, 2026; the email’s send date is not shown. Confirm current enrollment and final eligibility with the study team. Ongoing — per local email · JZP926-105"}, {"heading": "EpiFOS: cannabidiol oral solution for focal epilepsy", "anchor": "epifos-cannabidiol-oral-solution-for-focal-epilepsy", "text": "EpiFOS: cannabidiol oral solution for focal epilepsy Who to consider: focal epilepsy, ages 12–75. The supplied flyer specifies at least 3 countable focal seizures during the 6-week baseline, with at least 1 in each 3-week half. Design: open-label; all participants receive study drug, with no placebo. Six-week baseline, 16-week treatment, taper, and safety follow-up. The email reports 7 in-person visits including baseline. Initial screening: 1–4 ASMs; the email excludes rescue medications from the count. Willingness to abstain from marijuana; no Xcopri initiation within 4 months. A neurostimulation device must have been implanted at least 6 months earlier; the email specifies no changes in the preceding month, and the flyer specifies no planned setting changes. Additional flyer criteria: stable epilepsy treatments for at least 28 days; MRI within 10 years ruling out a progressive cause. Listed exclusions include LGS, status epilepticus within 3 months, and known lack of efficacy or poor tolerability with Epidiolex. Participant support: the email reports up to $800 for participants and up to $650 for qualifying caregivers’ time, plus transportation/accommodation support as needed. Confirm terms with the team. Sources: supplied email and EpiFOS flyer. Registry reference: NCT07233239 . This is a screening summary, not the full protocol. View EpiFOS source flyer Starting now — per local email · JZP926-203"}, {"heading": "EpiJME: cannabidiol capsules for JME myoclonic seizures", "anchor": "epijme-cannabidiol-capsules-for-jme-myoclonic-seizures", "text": "EpiJME: cannabidiol capsules for JME myoclonic seizures Who to consider: JME with uncontrolled myoclonic jerks; the flyer specifies age 10 or older and at least 4 myoclonic seizure days per 28 days in both historical and prospective baseline. Design: randomized 1:1, placebo-controlled; 28-day baseline followed by 16 weeks of treatment. Generalized tonic-clonic and absence seizures are tracked, but myoclonic seizure days drive eligibility and the primary endpoint, per the email. Initial screening: 1–4 stable ASMs, excluding rescue medications per the email; willingness to abstain from marijuana. No device implantation within 6 months and no changes in the preceding month. Additional flyer criteria: at least one prior failed treatment trial; medication/neurostimulation stability for at least 28 days. The flyer lists clobazam limits of 10 mg if under 30 kg and 20 mg if 30 kg or more; the dosing interval is not stated, so confirm with the study team. Timeline: the flyer describes 4-week titration and 12-week maintenance, followed by a 10-day taper and 2-week safety follow-up, with an optional 52-week open-label extension. Screening contact listed on flyer: Dr. Haris Rashid · haris.rashid@emory.edu . Contact Zeke for clinical or protocol-specific concerns. Sources: supplied email and EpiJME flyer. Registry reference: NCT07723963 . The public search result reviewed still listed “not yet recruiting”; confirm Emory’s current opening status with Zeke. View EpiJME source flyer Starting soon — per local email"}, {"heading": "Bluewing: zorevunersen for adults with Dravet syndrome", "anchor": "bluewing-zorevunersen-for-adults-with-dravet-syndrome", "text": "Bluewing: zorevunersen for adults with Dravet syndrome Zeke’s email describes an upcoming adult Dravet study of zorevunersen, an investigational RNA-targeting antisense therapy. Use this terminology rather than implying gene replacement. Who to flag: adults with Dravet syndrome who, with their families, would like to hear about the study—even if they have not decided whether to participate. Reported design: the email describes no placebo and intrathecal administration, with 10 administrations over 3 years. The Bluewing protocol and schedule were not independently confirmed; verify with Zeke. Study interest: the email highlights developmental outcomes as well as seizures. These are research aims, not established benefits. Final seizure-frequency and other eligibility criteria remain to be confirmed. Local study details: supplied email only. Background: Stoke Therapeutics pipeline . No Bluewing-specific public record was identified in the bounded source check. Possible future studies · Interest only"}, {"heading": "KCNT1 & CDKL5 watchlist", "anchor": "kcnt1-cdkl5-watchlist", "text": "KCNT1 & CDKL5 watchlist Zeke asks clinicians to flag patients with KCNT1- or CDKL5-related conditions for potential future opportunities. No named protocol, eligibility criteria, or confirmed opening date was supplied. Contact Zeke to discuss possible candidates."}]}, {"title": "Epilepsy Psychiatry Referral", "url": "manuals/psychiatry-referral/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Clinic Essentials · Psychiatry Referral Epilepsy Psychiatry Referral Route epilepsy patients to the dedicated Psychiatry pipeline in Epic."}, {"heading": "Place the referral in Epic", "anchor": "place-the-referral-in-epic", "text": "Place the referral in Epic 4 steps Search AMB REF PSYCH and select Ambulatory referral to Psychiatry The supplied screenshot shows this order, rather than the community Psychology referral. Choose provider Stephen Yang Set the reason to Other – Epilepsy Enter your clinical question. State the psychiatric concern, prior medication trial and response, and relevant ASM/seizure-control context. Include a PHQ-9 or GAD-7 if available; neither is required. Order selection is shown in the screenshot. Provider, reason, and clinical-question instructions are from the supplied internal email. View the supplied Epic order screenshot"}, {"heading": "Who fits this pathway?", "anchor": "who-fits-this-pathway", "text": "Who fits this pathway? Epilepsy patients with psychiatric comorbidities, especially: Persistent psychiatric symptoms or impaired functioning despite at least one medication trial at an adequate dose and duration. Psychiatric adverse effects from an epilepsy treatment, where psychiatric medication may help. Complex polypharmacy needing Psychiatry input to simplify and optimize treatment. PNES: this clinic does not see patients with PNES alone. Patients must also have epilepsy and an untreated or undertreated psychiatric condition."}, {"heading": "Make the referral goal specific", "anchor": "make-the-referral-goal-specific", "text": "Make the referral goal specific “Persistent mood disorder despite Celexa x 6 months, please assess for alternatives. Patient ASM regimen currently controlling seizures.” Example from the email; adapt to the patient’s actual history."}, {"heading": "What Psychiatry provides", "anchor": "what-psychiatry-provides", "text": "What Psychiatry provides Management through stabilization. Psychiatry treats psychiatric comorbidities and considers the consultation complete once the patient is stable. The email notes follow-up may extend to 1–2 years when needed; it is not limited to a single visit. Additional support stays with the epilepsy team. The email reports no Psychiatry social-work capacity for this pathway. Work/disability letters and other additional needs continue through the epilepsy team."}, {"heading": "Capacity & review status", "anchor": "capacity-review-status", "text": "Capacity & review status The email reported 15–20 new referrals per month for the next few months , with capacity subject to change. Source: internal email and Epic screenshot supplied September 24, 2026. This guide summarizes the supplied workflow; it is pending local review and is not approved institutional policy."}]}, {"title": "How to Clip Seizures", "url": "manuals/seizure-clipping-natus-2026/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "inpatient fellow Natus", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "← All manuals EMU Workflow Manual How to Clip Seizures Instructions for clipping and downloading seizure onset EDF files in Natus. Draft for colleague review — not approved institutional policy. Confirm this workflow against local policy and attending direction before use."}, {"heading": "Before you begin", "anchor": "before-you-begin", "text": "Before you begin Use authorized Natus access and a designated workstation. Do not interrupt clinical review of files. The source notes that remote access can be used to clip files, but downloading data requires connection to a major workstation."}, {"heading": "Clip the study", "anchor": "clip-the-study", "text": "Clip the study Prune the study of interest. Switch database to ICM and find the file of interest. You need to prune the start time after the patient's specific montage has been applied, so you know which electrode is which. To standardize our seizure clips, please identify the start time of the seizure and mark it precisely using the UEO label. Then go exactly 5 minutes (300 seconds) prior to the onset, which will be the start of the clip. The easiest way to do this is to select the UEO annotation in Natus, hit Ctrl+G, and set the time to 5 minutes EARLIER, while keeping the seconds the same. (e.g. 14:34:45 becomes 14:29:45) Click on to mark the start time of the clip. You will be prompted to name your clip. Please name the clip in the following fashion: szXX (e.g., sz01, sz02, sz14) Where XX is the sequential seizure number for that patient’s ICM admission. Do not clip or count subclinical/larval seizures for this purpose. Those can be labeled larval01, larval02 if you want to keep track similarly. Do not count mapping seizures in your sequential count. This standard format will allow us to process these files easily, as well as for better research. Now move to the offset of the seizure annotation (UEE). Similarly to above, once you click on it and highlight it, use Ctrl + G and move to 5 minutes AFTER the offset, and then click to mark the end of the clip. To make the actual clip, click on . Unclick video but make sure the epoch you want is selected under . Please unselect any other epochs that you have previously clipped, so you’re only clipping the epoch of note. One clip should contain one seizure and one seizure only. Adapted from How to Clip Seizures_2026.docx. Internal access instructions, staff contact information, and workstation locations are omitted from this review copy. The clipping instructions and toolbar icons are retained from the source."}]}, {"title": "Weekend On Call", "url": "manuals/weekend-no-np-orders-referrals/", "kind": "guide", "tags": "inpatient oncall fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "← All manuals EMU Operations Resource Weekend On Call Fellow checklist for Synergy orders and ReAct referrals when an NP is not available in the EMU. Draft for colleague review — not approved institutional policy. Use only for patients who meet the relevant program criteria. Confirm diagnoses, referral answers, orders, and discharge documentation against the patient record and current local workflow."}, {"heading": "Find the forms", "anchor": "find-the-forms", "text": "Find the forms Bulletin board Synergy order forms and two-page ReAct referrals are on the bulletin board for weekends and other days when no NP is available in the EMU. Open the Forms & Referrals directory for blank forms and preparation checklists. Synergy EEG order ReACT FND referral"}, {"heading": "Before handoff", "anchor": "before-handoff", "text": "Before handoff Complete and sign the relevant form using its checklist: Synergy EEG or ReACT FND . Then follow the local handoff below."}, {"heading": "Handoff and Faxing", "anchor": "handoff-and-faxing", "text": "Handoff and Faxing Usual workflow"}, {"heading": "Place completed forms on the NP desk", "anchor": "place-completed-forms-on-the-np-desk", "text": "Place completed forms on the NP desk The NP team will fax the completed order or referral Monday morning. When no NP around (Weekend/Weekdays)"}, {"heading": "Fax to the designated EMU scheduling coordinator", "anchor": "fax-to-the-designated-emu-scheduling-coordinator", "text": "Fax to the designated EMU scheduling coordinator When no NP is available, on weekends or weekdays, the fellow should fax the completed forms directly. Use the fax cover sheet and current fax number posted on the wall. Use the fax machine at the nurse’s station. On the hospital fax machine, dial 9 before the external fax number. The coordinator is arranging access to the EMU schedule so the patient can be looked up by name. The coordinator will add the non-clinical information and fax the order or referral with the required supporting material. The online ReACT form requests documentation confirming the FND diagnosis and the applicable diagnostic workup. The Synergy form requests clinical records and a copy of the insurance card. Or use Doximity Fax ↗"}, {"heading": "Patient Materials", "anchor": "patient-materials", "text": "Patient Materials PNES information and the ReAct handout. Parking vouchers, if needed; vouchers are valid only for visitor parking. Other available patient-education materials."}, {"heading": "Documentation Phrases", "anchor": "documentation-phrases", "text": "Documentation Phrases .dltpneseducation .PNEStherapy — provides information about the diagnosis and options for obtaining therapy."}, {"heading": "Doximity Fax", "anchor": "doximity-title", "text": "Doximity Fax Close Use your activated Doximity fax account in accordance with local patient-information policy. Send to the same intended recipient specified in the handoff above. Enter the recipient’s fax number directly. The leading 9 is for the hospital fax machine only."}, {"heading": "From the website", "anchor": "from-the-website", "text": "From the website Open Doximity Fax , sign in, and select New . Select the recipient by fax number or saved contact. Add cover-page text as needed. Use the paperclip Add control to attach the completed, signed forms and required records. Review the recipient and all pages, then select Send ."}, {"heading": "From iPhone or iPad", "anchor": "from-iphone-or-ipad", "text": "From iPhone or iPad In Doximity, open Fax , unlock the inbox, and tap New Fax . Enter the recipient in To . Check Cc EHR : a previously entered number persists until removed. Tap + to attach a file or scan the signed forms. Document scanning requires iOS 18 or later. Include all required supporting records. Review the recipient, attachments, and cover page; tap the send arrow. Review the fax in the Sent tab and check its delivery status. For image attachments, enable Contains Images (website: Attachments Contain Images ) to preserve image quality. Keep attachments within Doximity’s 50 MB limit. Official instructions: Website · iPhone / iPad · Android . Checked September 14, 2026."}]}, {"title": "ReACT FND Referral Form", "url": "forms/react-fnd/", "kind": "form", "tags": "form FND PNES referral", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Forms · FND referrals ReACT FND Referral Form Blank two-page referral form, completion checklist, and supporting documentation. Open blank PDF Official source Official source and blank PDF checked September 24, 2026. The linked form can change; review the current PDF before use. Training resource for colleague review. Confirm the patient-specific details and current program requirements before submitting."}, {"heading": "Complete the referral", "anchor": "complete-the-referral", "text": "Complete the referral Confirm that the patient meets the program criteria. In the existing EMU workflow, FND must be the clinically supported discharge diagnosis, rather than “spells.” Enter the patient’s name; review and correct every pre-entered answer. Sign the completed two-page referral. Include documentation confirming the FND diagnosis and the applicable diagnostic workup requested by the form."}, {"heading": "EMU handoff", "anchor": "emu-handoff", "text": "EMU handoff Follow the Weekend On Call handoff and faxing instructions when using this form in the EMU."}, {"heading": "Blank PDF text · page 1", "anchor": "blank-form", "text": "Version date: 05/13/2026 ReACT FND Health: PATIENT REFERRAL FORM PATIENT INFORMATION Name: ______________________________________________________________________ DOB _________________________ Contact Phone #: _______________________________ Email address: ________________________________________________ City/State: _____________________________________ REFERRING PROVIDER Name: ___________________________________________________ Clinic/Institution: __________________________________ Contact Phone #: ___________________ Fax #: _________________ HIPAA Secure email: ________________________________ CONSULTATION DETAILS Please describe the Functional Neurologic Disorder (FND) symptoms that they are experiencing: _________________________________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________________________________________ FND-diagnosing clinician name/specialty/institution (if different from referring): ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ NOTE: Neurological evaluation must be complete. Referrals for patients with pending evaluation will be declined. Please send a copy of the relevant documentation confirming the FND diagnosis. □Y □N FND diagnosis documented in at least one clinical note. Date of note/author: ___________________________ (required) □Y □N FND diagnosis discussed with patient. □Y □N Does the patient accept the referral? □Y □N Neurological exam shows “positive sign(s)” in accordance with the “incompatibility” criterion for diagnosis of FND. See for examples: Hallett M, Aybek S, Dworetzky BA, McWhirter L, Staab JP, Stone J. Functional neurological disorder: New subtypes and shared mechanisms. The Lancet Neurology, in press. DOI:https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422(21)00422-1. Positive signs/ date of note: ____________________________________________________________________________________ □Y □N Documentation of diagnostic workup, including EMG/NCS/MRI/CT/EEG where applicable is provided along with this referral form. Study type / date(s): _____________________________________________________________________________ __________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Patients with functional seizures/PNES: Date/duration of EEG _________________________________ Event captured: □Y □N Please list all known psychiatric and physical diagnoses: ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ COMORBID DIAGNOSES AND TREATMENT"}, {"heading": "Blank PDF text · page 2", "anchor": "blank-form", "text": "Version date: 05/13/2026 Are there any physical symptoms due to the other comorbid psychiatric or physical diagnoses that should not be targeted in treatment as functional symptoms: ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Please list all medications, include psychiatric and non-psychiatric medications: ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ Are any of these medications prescribed to treat FND (if yes, indicate above)? □ Y □ N REFERRING PROVIDER SIGNATURE Signature: _________________________________________________________ Date: ____________________ Submit the Referral Form using one of the following methods: Medical Documentation Portal Fax: 205-649-6824 To submit via the Medical Documentation Portal: 1. Save the completed form on your computer. 2. Click on the link above to access our secure upload medical documentation portal. 3. Upload the saved form at the link above. To submit via Fax: 4. Complete the form. 5. Send completed form to ReACT FND Health at 205-649-6824."}]}, {"title": "Synergy EEG Order Form", "url": "forms/synergy-eeg/", "kind": "form", "tags": "form ambulatory EEG video monitoring order insurance", "sections": [{"heading": "Overview", "anchor": "", "text": "Forms · EEG testing Synergy EEG Order Form Blank video monitoring EEG order form, preparation checklist, and supporting records. Open blank PDF Official source Official source and blank PDF checked September 24, 2026. The linked form can change; review the current PDF before use. Training resource for colleague review. Confirm the patient-specific details and current program requirements before submitting."}, {"heading": "Complete the order", "anchor": "complete-the-order", "text": "Complete the order Enter the patient’s name and the requested clinical orders. Complete the patient-specific fields and sign the order. Include the clinical records and a copy of the insurance card requested by the form."}, {"heading": "EMU handoff", "anchor": "emu-handoff", "text": "EMU handoff For weekends or other days without an NP, follow the Weekend On Call handoff and faxing instructions ."}, {"heading": "Blank PDF text · page 1", "anchor": "blank-form", "text": "PHONE: 954.909.0270 FAX: 844.889.8133 WWW.SYNERGYEEG.COM VIDEO MONITORING EEG ORDER FORM REPRESENTATIVE: ① PATIENT DEMOGRAPHICS PATIENT NAME EMAIL ADDRESS PATIENT PHONE PATIENT CELL DATE OF BIRTH PATIENT ADDRESS CITY, STATE, ZIP PRIMARY INSURANCE INSURANCE ID # GROUP # SECONDARY INSURANCE INSURANCE ID # GROUP # ② CLINICAL HISTORY — MEDICARE ACCEPTED ICD-10 CODES — CHECK ALL THAT APPLY F44.4 Conversion disorder (motor) G40.109 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (simple partial), not intractable, w/o status G40.501 Seizures (external causes, non-intractable, status) F44.5 Conversion disorder (seizures/convulsions) G40.111 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (simple partial), intractable, w/ status G40.509 Seizures (external causes, non-intractable) F44.6 Conversion disorder (sensory) G40.119 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (simple partial), intractable, w/o status G40.801 Other epilepsy (non-intractable, status) F44.7 Conversion disorder (mixed) G40.201 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (complex partial), not intractable, w/ status G40.802 Other epilepsy (non-intractable) G40.A01 Absence epilepsy, not intractable, w/ status epilepticus G40.209 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (complex partial), not intractable, w/o status G40.803 Other epilepsy (intractable, status) G40.A09 Absence epilepsy, not intractable, w/o status epilepticus G40.211 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (complex partial), intractable, w/ status G40.804 Other epilepsy (intractable) G40.A11 Absence epilepsy, intractable, w/ status epilepticus G40.219 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (complex partial), intractable, w/o status G40.89 Other seizures G40.A19 Absence epilepsy, intractable, w/o status epilepticus G40.301 Generalized idiopathic epilepsy, not intractable, w/ status G40.901 Epilepsy, unspecified (non-intractable, status) G40.001 Localization-related idiopathic epilepsy, not intractable, w/ status G40.309 Generalized idiopathic epilepsy, not intractable, w/o status G40.909 Epilepsy, unspecified (non-intractable) G40.009 Localization-related idiopathic epilepsy, not intractable, w/o status G40.311 Generalized idiopathic epilepsy, intractable, w/ status G40.911 Epilepsy, unspecified (intractable, status) G40.011 Localization-related idiopathic epilepsy, intractable, w/ status G40.319 Generalized idiopathic epilepsy, intractable, w/o status G40.919 Epilepsy, unspecified (intractable) G40.019 Localization-related idiopathic epilepsy, intractable, w/o status R55 Syncope / Collapse G40.821 Epileptic spasms (non-intractable, status) G40.101 Focal symptomatic epilepsy (simple partial), not intractable, w/ status R56.1 Post-traumatic seizures G40.822 Epileptic spasms (non-intractable) G40.812 Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, not intractable, without status epilepticus R56.9 Unspecified convulsions G40.823 Epileptic spasms (intractable, status) G40.813 Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, intractable, with status epilepticus G40.814 Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, intractable, without status epilepticus G40.824 Epileptic spasms (intractable) ③ PROCEDURE ORDERED — LONG-TERM VIDEO AMBULATORY EEG LENGTH OF MONITORING 48 HRS 72 HRS 96 HRS 120 HRS 144 HRS 168 HRS EKG ✓ Included I confirm my acknowledgment that an EKG is included and will be performed concurrently with the AEEG as a standard component of testing. Previous EEG: Yes No If yes, date of EEG: If previous EEG is not indicated, a routine EEG (95816) will be provided if required by payer. ④ ORDERING PHYSICIAN INFORMATION PHYSICIAN NAME NPI # PHYSICIAN ADDRESS CITY, STATE, ZIP PHYSICIAN PHONE PHYSICIAN FAX EMAIL Physician Statement: I certify that I am referring the above named patient for long-term electroencephalographic (EEG) Monitoring or video long-term EEG monitoring as listed above and I certify that I am referring the above named patient for long-term electroencephalographic (EEG) Monitoring or video long-term EEG monitoring as listed above and to the best of my knowledge this test is medically necessary in order to diagnose the patient. PHYSICIAN SIGNATURE DATE ORDERED PLEASE FAX THIS FORM ALONG WITH CLINICALS AND COPY OF INSURANCE CARD TO 844.889.8133"}]}, {"title": "Electrical Stimulation Mapping SOP", "url": "manuals/electrical-stimulation-mapping-sop/pdf/Electrical%20Stimulation%20Mapping%20SOP.pdf", "kind": "pdf", "tags": "inpatient sEEG stim fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Page 1", "anchor": "page=1", "text": "EMORY EPILEPSY CENTER Department of Neurology Emory University Electrical Stimulation Mapping during Intracranial EEG Recordings Policy and Procedure (v1.0) This policy's purpose is to motivate and explain the electrical stimulation mapping procedure in patients with epilepsy undergoing intracranial EEG monitoring. Background and rationale When cortical stimulation is performed in patients with epilepsy following standard protocols, it induces seizures in 34-57% of patients. The presence of stimulation-induced seizures and resecting electrode contacts implicated in the stimulation-induced seizure onset zone are both correlated with good surgical outcomes. Thus, performing cortical stimulation following a standard protocol may add useful ancillary information for surgical planning (Oderiz et al., 2019; Jobst et al., 2020; Trébuchon et al., 2021). Stimulation-induced seizures are more likely when the patient’s last spontaneous seizure was >24 hours prior to stimulation (Oderiz et al., 2019; Jobst et al., 2020). Stimulation-induced seizures are more common with high frequency (50 Hz) stimulation than with low frequency (1 Hz) stimulation. However, atypical seizures are more common with high frequency stimulation (7.8%) than with low frequency stimulation (1.5%) (Oderiz et al., 2019). The above data support performing both low- and high-frequency stimulation. In particular, the following protocol involves performing low frequency stimulation on most contacts prior to weaning anti-seizure medications to maximize the probability of inducing a seizure using an unbiased approach. Then, if no seizures are induced with low frequency stimulation and the epilepsy localization remains uncertain following capture of spontaneous seizures (or if needed for functional mapping), high frequency stimulation should be performed targeting hypothesized seizure onset zone contacts (and relevant eloquent cortex, in the case of functional mapping). This targeted approach to high frequency stimulation minimizes the risk of capturing atypical seizures. Timing of stimulation Low frequency stimulation should be performed for all cases prior to weaning anti-seizure medications. If the patient is on a long-acting anti-seizure medication at home and this medication was weaned prior to EMU admission, stimulation may be performed in the setting of this wean. Ideally, stimulation should be performed the morning of the second day of intracranial EEG recording according to clinical team availability. This timing is late enough to allow clinicians to capture spontaneous interictal EEG prior to stimulation, and early enough to not interfere with the timing of medication wean. We expect that the low frequency stimulation procedure will take approximately 2 hours (assuming 100 contact pairs are stimulated). High frequency stimulation should only be performed under the following circumstances: Stimulation is needed to map eloquent cortex OR No seizures were induced with low frequency stimulation AND the epilepsy localization remains uncertain after the remainder of the intracranial EEG evaluation."}, {"heading": "Page 2", "anchor": "page=2", "text": "High frequency stimulation should be performed after collecting spontaneous seizures and once the patient has been restarted on home medications. Ideally, it should be performed at least 1 day prior to explant. We expect that high frequency stimulation will take approximately 1 hour (assuming 20-30 contact pairs are stimulated). Setting Up Intravenous lorazepam (2 mg) should be available in the room, and the patient should have a working IV. Equipment needed for the procedure is the Nicolet Cortical Stimulator box with external power cable and red and black Daisy Chain cable. Plug the power cable for stimulator box into wall outlet and turn the power button on. The Daisy Chain connects to the top of stimulator box via the black and red plugs on one end and the 6 plug female connector plugs into the Natus Quantum box- marked by a lightning bolt. If 2 Quantum break-out boxes are in use, utilize the splitter cable and plug into both “lightning bolt” inputs. See the Figures at the end of the protocol for a schematic. Using the Natus computer in the room with patient study pulled up à Highlight Controls à DSM Stimulation. This opens the stimulator controls and allows to choose 2 electrodes to stimulate between through a drop-down menu. After choosing the designated 2 electrodes click on the Green check (this opens the pathway for stimulation). When finished click Red “X” to change the designated electrodes. Continue with electrode pairs given by the physician for stimulation. Stimulation Team The following tasks need to be performed in stimulation: - Stimulation task: Setup and breakdown of the stimulator. Adjust stimulation parameters and deliver stimulation - EEG task: Monitor the EEG for seizures and after discharges, and adjust stimulation contacts - Clinical task: Monitor the patient for clinical changes and administer IV Ativan if needed - Recording task: Transcribe Electrode Stimulation sheet (electrodes stimulated, current, frequency, duration, task and effect). Prepare and complete the official Stimulation Report. At least two team members should always be present in the room to perform all stimulation tasks. The epilepsy attending should always monitor the stimulation session. It is up to the attending’s discretion whether to monitor stimulation from the patient room or remotely in the EMU workroom. Choosing electrodes to stimulate For low frequency stimulation, all adjacent pairs of electrode contacts should be stimulated including white matter contacts, with the following exceptions: - Contacts outside the brain or adjacent to the meninges - Contacts with significant artifact or abnormal impedance - Contacts believed likely to produce a noxious sensation with the planned stimulation parameters For high frequency stimulation , only those electrode contacts hypothesized to be the seizure onset zone or in eloquent cortex (for functional mapping) should be stimulated. The same electrode exclusion criteria apply as with low frequency stimulation. For both low and high frequency stimulation, the contacts thought most likely to be the seizure onset zone should be stimulated last to avoid inducing a large seizure early in the stimulation session. However, because LFS occurs prior to capturing spontaneous seizures, there is no reliable way of predicting which contacts will yield ictal patterns. Although it is encouraged to collect data from all contact pairs, it is up to"}, {"heading": "Page 3", "anchor": "page=3", "text": "the attending’s discretion to continue LFS once a seizure has been induced. Use of the ictal disrupt button is highly encouraged. Stimulation protocol Make sure Natus is annotating the stimulation contacts and parameters. The annotation should read “Start stimulation from X to Y ([parameters])” Low frequency stimulation - Setup: Probe Biphasic - Pulse Duration: 500 us - 1 Hz frequency - 30 second train duration - 3 mA current - Wait until the EEG normalizes post-stimulation before proceeding to the next contact pair High frequency stimulation and functional mapping - Setup: Probe Biphasic - Pulse Duration: 500 us - 50 Hz frequency - 5-6 second train duration - Increase current from 1-4 mA in 1 mA steps in the neocortical contacts, and 1-3 mA in 1 mA steps in the mesial temporal contacts. Per attending discretion, stimulation could instead be performed at 4 mA in the neocortical contacts (3 mA in the mesial temporal contacts) without gradual increase. - Per attending discretion, higher currents may be used provided they remain under the established safety limits of charge density in order to avoid tissue injury (see safety table below). - Wait until the EEG normalizes post-stimulation before proceeding to the next current - Special care should be taken when performing high frequency stimulation in the following circumstances: - Primary motor cortex: this may produce a strong noxious stimulus - Mesial temporal cortex, Heschl’s gyrus, and dysplastic cortex: seizures and afterdischarges can be induced by high frequency stimulation with lower currents in these regions (Oderiz et al., 2019; Trébuchon and Chauvel, 2016). Seizures induced in these regions with high frequency stimulation should therefore be interpreted with particular caution Testing functional cortex When performing high frequency stimulation to hypothesized regions of eloquent cortex, assess for functional changes using the following approach: For hypothesized receptive or productive language areas: - Have the patient read a block of text aloud (starting stimulation 2-3 sentences into the text) - If baseline reading ability is impaired, use counting/reciting familiar lines instead - If the patient has speech arrest, ask them the reason for the arrest (to rule out other causes such as pain) For hypothesized visual cortex: - Place a 16x22 inch sheet of paper 50 cm in front of the patient, with horizontal and vertical lines across the midline and a 3 mm fixation point at the intersection - Ask the patient to look at the fixation point during stimulus - Trace the visual response location on the sheet of paper and record the description"}, {"heading": "Page 4", "anchor": "page=4", "text": "For hypothesized sensorimotor cortex: - Note that complaints of dysesthesias may be subtle motor rather than sensory phenomena (in which case, a higher current will likely elicit a motor response) - To test for a negative motor response, ask the patient to perform a sustained motor task (such as finger tapping for hypothesized hand motor cortex) General protocol Both the patient and the EEG should be monitored throughout each stimulation for seizures, afterdischarges, or other clinical effects. The stimulation current should be documented on Natus. All stimulation parameters and their electroclinical effects should be documented in the Epic report. End stimulation at a given pair of contacts if it induces a seizure or if it produces an uncomfortable sensation. The “Stop” button can be pressed to abort the 30-second train of low frequency stimulation. The patient’s seizure button should be pressed for any seizure both for documentation purposes and to alert the remainder of the clinical team to enter the room for additional support. If an electroclinical seizure is prolonged (>30 seconds), the “Ictal Disrupt” button should be pressed. If the seizure continues despite Ictal Disrupt x 2-3 or if the seizure progresses to a convulsion, IV lorazepam should be administered. If a convulsive seizure is induced, then stimulation should be stopped for the day. Stimulation may proceed at the remaining contacts if a focal aware or focal impaired awareness seizure is induced at the attending’s discretion based on the specific clinical situation. The clinical team should determine whether additional stimulation is needed if the stimulation session is stopped early due to a seizure. Reports The epilepsy fellow should document the report in Epic using “.corticalstimreport”. Billing To bill for cortical stimulation, use one of the paper bills (similar to those used to bill for EEG reading in the EMU). Ask the lead EMU technician for a paper bill. Use the following CPT codes: - 95961 (Functional cortical and subcortical mapping by stimulation and/or recording of electrodes on brain surface, first hour) - 95962 (Functional cortical and subcortical mapping by stimulation and/or recording of electrodes on brain surface, each addt'l hour)) – this can be billed once you’ve crossed 31 minutes. For example, mapping starts 10AM and ends at 12:34PM. The attending will bill: 95961 x 1 (10:00-11:00) + 95962 x 2 (11:00-12:00 & 12:00-12:34) Billing is based on day of service. If a mapping session is paused and the team returns at a later time to complete the session the same day, add up the time within the day (i.e., cannot bill 95961 twice on the same day). LFS and HFS sessions are billed as separate encounters, so there will be two separate 95961 if both sessions are performed. Safety parameters to avoid local tissue injury The primary metric that predicts local tissue injury from electrical cortical stimulation is the charge density: Charge density ( μC cm2 ) = Stimulation intensity ( mA ) × Pulse duration ( ms ) Electrode surface area ( cm2 )"}, {"heading": "Page 5", "anchor": "page=5", "text": "Two reported safety limits for charge density are \u003c30 mC/cm2 (McCreery et al., 1990) and \u003c55 mC/cm2 (Gordon et al., 1990). The following table shows the calculated charge densities for Penn’s current AdTech depth electrodes (as of February 2022): References Gordon, Barry, et al. \"Parameters for direct cortical electrical stimulation in the human: histopathologic confirmation.\" Electroencephalography and clinical neurophysiology 75.5 (1990): 371-377. Jobst, Barbara C., et al. \"Intracranial EEG in the 21st Century.\" Epilepsy currents 20.4 (2020): 180-188. McCreery, Douglas B., et al. \"Charge density and charge per phase as cofactors in neural injury induced by electrical stimulation.\" IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering 37.10 (1990): 996- 1001. Oderiz, Carolina Cuello, et al. \"Association of cortical Stimulation–Induced seizure with surgical outcome in patients with focal drug-resistant epilepsy.\" JAMA neurology 76.9 (2019): 1070-1078. Trébuchon, Agnes, et al.” Electrical stimulation for seizure induction during SEEG exploration: a useful predictor of postoperative seizure recurrence?” J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry 92 (2021);22-26. Trébuchon, Agnès, and Patrick Chauvel. \"Electrical stimulation for seizure induction and functional mapping in stereoelectroencephalography.\" Journal of Clinical Neurophysiology 33.6 (2016): 511-521."}, {"heading": "Page 6", "anchor": "page=6", "text": "Photos of stimulator setup Front view of the Nicolet stimulator: Diagram of stimulator showing all connections: Power button and connection to power cable: Top of box with red and black outputs to Natus breakout box (blue output on left remains unplugged)"}, {"heading": "Page 7", "anchor": "page=7", "text": "Natus breakout box (usually in bed with the patient). Plug the cable coming from the Nicolet stimulator to the lightning bolt input (if there are two breakout boxes then you need to plug one into each breakout box):"}]}, {"title": "Thalamic Protocol", "url": "manuals/thalamic-protocol/pdf/Thalamic%20Protocol.pdf", "kind": "pdf", "tags": "inpatient sEEG stim fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "Page 1", "anchor": "page=1", "text": "EMORY UNIVERSITY Epilepsy & Clinical Neurophysiology Setting Things Up: BEFORE you plan to do the thalamic trial, follow the steps to set up the stimulation: 1. Stop Current Study (You can click the x on the RIGHT upper corner) 2. Click “Review Study” in the study list 3. Click “Edit” 4. “Settings” 5. Click “Acquisition” tab 6. Click Advanced 7. Uncheck 1) enable digital trigger , 2) use Nicolet cortical stim 8. OK -> Apply -> Save the changes 9. Return to the study You will pick the target contacts on the Natus -> DSM control, but adjust parameters, and perform continuous stim on the Nicolet. 1. On Nicolet, change the duration to “continuous” 2. Use a timer to keep time (30 seconds, 20 mins, 1 hour) 3. IF you do not see stim artifact on EEG, click “OPEN/CLOSE relay”, or “START” on the DSM control THALAMIC short trial Recommended for: hippocampal onsets with abundant spiking; FCD with nearly continuous repetitive spiking - Set stim (100 Hz, 100us) - Start at 1mA and stimulate for one minute to test tolerability - Increase by 1mA to reach 6mA, 30 seconds at a time - Leave it running on continuous for 20 minutes (can decrease to 5-10 minutes for FCD with continuous spiking if the effect is immediately obvious) - Turn it off for 5 minutes before trialing another contact pair"}, {"heading": "Page 2", "anchor": "page=2", "text": "- If time allows, consider testing 5 Hz stimulation with 200 us pulse width up to 4mA (this could help determine patients that can be set to low frequency to avoid mood/cognition side effects) THALAMIC prolonged trial Recommended for: broad network onsets; quantitatively testing pre vs. post CCEPs - Set stim at 100 Hz, 100us - Start at 1mA and stimulate for one minute to test tolerability - Increase by 1mA to reach 6mA, one minute at a time - Leave it running on continuous for 120 minutes - Turn it off for 15 minutes before trialing another contact pair How to stim effect? o Ideally, we will use a spike detector to quantify pre and post. In the absence of this pipeline for the time being, if the effect is not apparent, use the recommended workflow below: o Determine your baseline interictal spiking rate (choose 3 x 10’ baseline samples and count spikes per channel on main channels, average the spikes across all three epochs) o Determine the spiking rate on the 10’ epoch between the post-stim 5 min mark and before the start of the next trial (e.g. if LPUL1-2 was stimulated at 10:03 for 120 minutes and turned off at 12:03, ignore 12:04-12:09, and count spikes between 12:40 to 12:50). o Repeat for each trial."}]}, {"title": "Cortical Stimulation Manual", "url": "https://erafat.github.io/cortical-stimulation-manual-review/", "kind": "external", "tags": "inpatient sEEG stim fellow", "sections": [{"heading": "External manual · title and summary only", "anchor": "", "text": "Comprehensive 17-chapter reading edition on cortical stimulation during SEEG: anatomy, responses, interpretation, and source-linked teaching. Draft for colleague review; clinical signoff pending."}]}];
